// XFAIL: RT-262: a `&mut [i64]` behind a `&` made into `*mut [i64]` at a tuple element, a generic call argument (`Vec::push`, `id::<*mut [i64]>`) or a closure's result is accepted, and a write through `*const [i64]` is too: a mutable access through a shared reference (0.30.2)
// From the review of g302bor (a/v03, v04, f67, v07, f63). RT-250's element and
// variant beside: `*const [T]` and `*mut [T]` are one type, and the checker
// records a view's mutable coercion only at let, assign, return, a field, a
// `*mut [T]` parameter and a cast (build.vl raw_mut_goal; lower_arg's view
// branch reslices shared for a parameter `T = *mut [i64]`). The last form
// writes through `h.s as *const [i64]` (the cast is rustc's too).
// rustc: E0596 at the first four (cannot borrow `*h.s` as mutable, as it is
// behind a `&` reference), E0594 at the write (cannot assign to `p[_]`, which
// is behind a `*const` pointer). On v0.30.1 too.
// ERROR: line 27
// ERROR: line 33
// ERROR: line 38
// ERROR: line 42
// ERROR: line 51
// ERROR-COUNT: 5
// ERROR: error[borrowck.borrow_immutable]
struct H<'a> {
    s: &'a mut [i64],
}

fn id<T>(t: T) -> T {
    t
}

fn tuple(h: &H) -> *mut [i64] {
    let t: (*mut [i64], i64) = (h.s, 1i64);
    t.0
}

fn pushed(h: &H, gpa: Gpa) -> *mut [i64] {
    let mut v: Vec<*mut [i64], Gpa> = Vec::new_in(gpa);
    v.push(h.s);
    v[0]
}

fn generic(h: &H) -> *mut [i64] {
    id::<*mut [i64]>(h.s)
}

fn closure(h: &H) -> *mut [i64] {
    let mut c = || -> *mut [i64] { h.s };
    c()
}

fn through_const(h: &H) {
    let p = h.s as *const [i64];
    // UNSAFE: the case: a write through a `*const [i64]`
    // SAFETY: none; the write must be refused
    unsafe {
        (*p)[0] = 9;
    }
}

fn main(gpa: Gpa) {
    let mut x = [1i64, 2];
    let h = H { s: &mut x };
    let ps = [tuple(&h), pushed(&h, gpa), generic(&h), closure(&h)];
    through_const(&h);
    println("{} {}", ps.len(), h.s[0]);
}
